          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em topological insulator
            (TI) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} quantum material
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase of matter
            where:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                as in an ordinary
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
                separates the filled
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bands"} valence bands
                from the unocuupied
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conduction+bands"} conduction bands
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energies"} energies
            %li
              %p
                in addition the topological
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} class
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                over the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unstable+classification+of+topological+phases"} unstable classification of topological phases
                or, more coarsely, in the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
                ).
          %p
            The second condition means that a topological insulator locally (over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystal
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momenta"} momenta
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
          %p
            Of course, real samples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
            are not ideal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystals"} crystals
            in that they typically have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenological
            property of topological insulators is thought to be that at their sample boundaries, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            closes (so that there the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            is no longer a well-defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbundle"} subbundle
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch+bundle"} Bloch bundle
            there), making their boundary a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductor+%28electromagnetism%29"} conductor
            in which characteristic “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edge+modes"}> edge modes
            ” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currents"} currents
            propagate (“bulk-boundary correspondence”).
          %p
            Moreover, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch+Hamiltonians"} Bloch Hamiltonians
            of real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystals"} crystals
            and their accessible deformations are typically constrained to respect some of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+group"} point group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+group"} space group
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
            (in addition to possible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PCT+symmetries"} PCT symmetries
            such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+reversal+symmetry"} time reversal symmetry
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+crystalline+insulators"} topological crystalline insulators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            is then a class in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+theory"} equivariant homotopy theory
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+crystalline+insulator"} topological crystalline insulator
            is non-trivial as long as such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+group"} point group
            symmetries are respected by all of its deformation, but would be trivial otherwise, then one speaks of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"} symmetry protected topological phase
          %p
            In experimental practice, topological insulators are often engineered by starting with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+semimetal"} topological semimetal
            — where a bulk
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            does close over higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifolds"} submanifolds
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/band+nodes"}> band nodes
            ”) — and then deforming it to make the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch+Hamiltonian"} Bloch Hamiltonian
            pick up “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+terms"}> mass terms
            ” which “open the gap” at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/band+nodes"} band nodes
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "mass+term#ExamplesInTopologicalPhasesOfMatter"} there
